\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An integral coefficient with two pairs}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For #{V=\mathbb Z^2} and #{K=2\bbI}, the
[ring paired coefficient](fdip-00TJ) extracts the coefficient
of #{\Omega_V=\psi_1\bar\psi_1\psi_2\bar\psi_2}. Directly,
##{\extexp(\theta_K)
 =(1+2\psi_1\bar\psi_1)(1+2\psi_2\bar\psi_2),
 \qquad \mathcal I_V\extexp(\theta_K)=4.}
The same product makes sense after reduction modulo any integer.
For #{K=\bbI} over #{\mathbb F_2}, its top coefficient is one,
as in \ref{fdip-00TG}.}
